    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d275-a1">

                <pb facs="0477" n="383" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg383"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 195.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Colman to Mr. Bayard</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of Agriculture</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington, D. C.</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-01-05"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 5,
                        1887</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I am frequently asked by correspondents who
                    desire to ship plants to Germany in regard to the construction of the German
                    laws governing such importation, and within a few days the following question
                    has arisen: It is understood that a certificate to the effect that the plants to
                    be imported have not been grown near potatoes or grapes is required; what is the
                    form of this certificate, and by whom should it be indorsed?</p>
                <p>I should like to procure through your Department, as soon as possible, an
                    authoritative answer to this query, and I should also esteem it a favor if you
                    can secure for me copies of the whole law governing this traffic.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Norman J. Colman</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Commissioner.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
